**AI assistants sent 2.1% of sessions across a 20-site sample over 90 days, 3.4% once one bot-skewed site is excluded; ChatGPT sent more of that traffic than every other assistant combined, and the per-site share ranged from 0% to 10.7%.** Treat the range as the finding.

I run analytics on around 115 GA4 properties. This data comes from a 20-site sample pulled directly from GA4 on 18 September 2026, covering the 90 days before that date. It is not a full-portfolio census, and the sites sampled were not chosen at random.

## What share of traffic comes from AI search? {% #findings %}

AI assistants sent 4,372 of 205,321 sessions across the 20-site sample, a 2.1% share. Exclude one site with an anomalous traffic pattern and the share rises to 3.4%.

That one site, catchment.school, recorded 98,770 sessions in the window, 84,957 of them logged as "(direct)". A single education-niche site generating that much direct traffic without a matching level of referral or search traffic does not match normal visitor behaviour. I flagged it as likely bot or crawler contamination and excluded it from the 3.4% figure. It did send 766 AI-referral sessions itself (716 ChatGPT, 50 Copilot), the third-highest raw count in the sample, which is why excluding the whole site changes the aggregate less than the "(direct)" number alone would suggest.

